  "content": {
    "id": "p4-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Let $n>1$ be an integer. Prove that $\\log_n(n+1)$ is not a rational number.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Irrational.\n\nIf $\\log_n(n+1)=\\frac{a}{b}$ for positive integers $a,b$, then $n^a=(n+1)^b$, which is impossible because one side is even and the other is odd.",
      "worked": "Suppose $\\log_n(n+1)=\\dfrac{a}{b}$ for positive integers $a,b$. The numerator and denominator can be chosen positive because $n>1$ and $n+1>1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_n(n+1)&=\\frac{a}{b}\\\\n^{a/b}&=n+1\\\\(n^{a/b})^b&=(n+1)^b\\\\n^a&=(n+1)^b\\end{align*}$$\n\nIf $n$ is even, $n^a$ is even and $(n+1)^b$ is odd. If $n$ is odd, $n^a$ is odd and $(n+1)^b$ is even. Either case contradicts the equality. Therefore $\\log_n(n+1)$ is irrational.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
